Subject: [PATCH] canvas: noise moves blended pixels and their alpha

The readback noise moved only colour, by one step, on any pixel at a
colour edge. An alpha-only hash or a hash of colours rounded to 16 steps
was then the same for every profile, and a canvas of solid rectangles
read back with changed edge pixels, which shows the noise.

Now only a pixel that is a blend of its neighbours moves: a colour none
of its eight neighbours has, inside the range they span. That is what
anti-aliasing leaves on curved and sloped edges and in text. Its colour
and its alpha each move by up to two steps and stay inside that range,
so the pixel is still a plausible blend. Solid fills, hard edges,
straight half-pixel edges and lone dots read back exactly as drawn.

The step depends on the pixel's position on the canvas, not in the
returned rectangle, and getImageData reads a one-pixel border inside the
canvas to decide its edge pixels, so a read of part of a canvas agrees
with a read of all of it and with toDataURL and toBlob. A read that
reaches past the canvas no longer fills the outside with unset memory.

